<article class="env-remark" id="01M9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01M9">Remark <span data-tag="01M9">27.8.5</span>.</a> The map from $M_0$ to the global sections of $\widetilde M$ is generally far from being an isomorphism. A trivial example is to take $S = k[x, y, z]$ with $1 = \deg (x) = \deg (y) = \deg (z)$ (or any number of variables) and to take $M = S/(x^{100}, y^{100}, z^{100})$. It is easy to see that $\widetilde M = 0$, but $M_0 = k$. </p>
